From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 22:02:38 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 079/148] hjviewer: an optional app lock on phones (Face ID,
 fingerprint or passcode)

W13 (TV40): Settings on the phone and tablet app get a Security section
with an "App lock" switch (off by default) and, while it is on, how long
the app may stay in the background before it asks again (immediately,
1 minute by default, 5 minutes). Turning the switch on or off takes one
successful authentication; a device with neither biometrics nor a
passcode cannot turn it on and is told why.

While on, the app asks at a cold start and when it comes back after at
least the chosen time. The cover sits above the navigator in the app's
builder, so routes, dialogs and toasts stay under it; a remote session
keeps running, but no pointer, key, focus, semantics or back button
reaches it while locked. It asks once by itself when it comes up and
again with [Unlock]. A device that lost its passcode is not kept out:
the lock turns off with a notice.

Authentication is local_auth's (biometrics with the device passcode as
the fallback). The app takes local_auth 2.3.0's Android and iOS
packages and its platform interface rather than the app-facing package,
which would also build local_auth_windows (NuGet at build time) into
the Windows app. Only the switch and the time are stored, as local
options; desktops have no app lock.

Android: MainActivity extends FlutterFragmentActivity, which the
biometric prompt needs (activity/context references become this), and
NormalTheme is an AppCompat theme for Android 8 and below. iOS:
NSFaceIDUsageDescription in Info.plist.

The decisions are plain Dart (hjviewer_applock_rules.dart) with tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
